    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:3rem">
      <h2>What the walk-through covers</h2>
      <p>When the video lands, it walks through the items below in order. In the meantime, you can mentally walk through them now using the demo sites at <a href="/samples/" class="inline-link">/samples/</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <ol class="walkthrough-list">
      <li>
        <strong>The homepage.</strong>
        We look at the hero, the value proposition, the trust signals, and the call-to-action sitting above the fold, along with what loads on the page, in what order, and how quickly all of it arrives.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A service page.</strong>
        We see how a single service is structured: an outcome-focused headline, a clear list of what is included, the three questions the customer is actually asking, and schema markup that quietly wires the page to the local pack.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A service-area page.</strong>
        We talk about why one genuinely local city page tends to beat ten generic doorway pages, by way of real local content, a real local map, and references to the neighborhoods you actually work in.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The contact form.</strong>
        Rather than the standard name-email-message form, we look at trade-specific intake: the four or five questions that pre-qualify a lead before it ever lands in your inbox.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The pricing page.</strong>
        We see how transparent pricing changes the conversation you are having with the customer, and why posted prices tend to increase the number of qualified inquiries you receive rather than decrease it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The blog.</strong>
        Blogs are optional, and we look at how one earns long-tail organic traffic over time and how the maintenance burden actually breaks down once it is in place.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The dark-mode toggle, the search, the sticky table of contents, and the analytics dashboard.</strong>
        These are the quality-of-life features that take a site from passable to genuinely used by the people who land on it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Lighthouse run.</strong>
        We run a live PageSpeed score on every page in front of you. Most owners have never seen a 95+ run on their own URL, and once they do, the comparison to whatever they had before tends to be difficult to un-see.
      </li>
    </ol>

